  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to improve upon the current upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by employing a multi-stage optimization strategy. Unlike the previous algorithm that primarily relied on binary representations and local hill climbing, we will use a combination of global exploration via genetic algorithms and local refinement with constrained optimization. This approach aims to escape local optima by promoting diversity while maintaining focused exploitation through mathematical optimization techniques. The key innovation is the use of a wavelet-based initialization to capture both periodic and aperiodic patterns, complemented by surrogate modeling to reduce computational cost.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- We represent the step function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] discretely with n=360 samples at step size dx=2/360.\n- The objective is to minimize C5 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx, computed by correlation and scaled by dx.\n- Constraints:\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  - Sum(h) = 180 exactly (n/2)\n- Objective function: C5 = max(product) over all time lags.\n- Variable transformation: We use a wavelet transform for initialization while maintaining feasibility.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialization:\n      i. Validate n=360 and target sum=180\n      ii. Initialize population using wavelet-based decomposition (initial candidates have normalized sum)\n      iii. Generate diversification candidates via Fourier transform perturbations\n\n   b. Multi-stage optimization:\n      Stage 1 (Global exploration):\n        - Genetic algorithm with tournament selection, wavelet crossover, and targeted mutation\n        - Evaluate candidates using fast correlation with clamped boundaries\n        - Track non-dominated solutions per time lag\n\n      Stage 2 (Intensive refinement):\n        - Transform promising candidates to continuous domain via constrained projection\n        - Optimize using SLSQP with quadratic surrogate model\n        - Implement derivative-free line search for derivative checking\n\n   c. Trust region strategy:\n      i. For each candidate, set interval bounds through constraint-based trust region\n      ii. Use finite difference gradient estimation with dynamic step size\n      iii. Update feasibility region based on constraint violations\n\n   d. Final adjustment:\n      - Use dithering to escape local minima\n      - Project to feasible set using greedy sparse adjustment\n      - Ensures exact sum of 180 with minimal perturbation\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   a. Algorithm selection:\n      - Primary: Wavelet population initialization + GA exploration + SLSQP refinement\n      - Secondary: Fourier-aware diversification, dithering post-refinement\n   b. Parameter tuning:\n      - Wavelet detail level: 5-7 (start wavelet scale at 0.1, adaptive decay rate 0.95)\n      - Population size: 20-50 (reduce by half each stage)\n      - Mutation rate: 0.1-0.3 (depends on improvement)\n      - Trust region expansion: Factor of 1.5-2.0\n   c. Dynamic adjustments:\n      - Stage transitions when improvement stalls (30%+ reduction in best C5)\n      - Function evaluation reduction through surrogate modeling (use 20% of the evaluations with gradient-only confidence checks)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   a. Stage timing:\n      - Exploration: 1/3 budget (focus on lower C5 values)\n      - Refinement: 2/3 budget (focus on local search)\n      - Check deada deadlines before optimizing expensive time lags (>=10^5 shifts)\n   b. Deadline handling:\n      - Use early termination for non-improving candidates\n      - Maintain best known candidate throughout\n      - On timeout, return current best with exact adjustment\n   c. Stability measures:\n      - Progressive evaluation: First check short lags (<=n/4) then longer ones (using interval estimates)\n      - Atomic updates with fail-safe projection\n      - Abandon and restart candidates showing excessive constraint violations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   a. Feasibility checks:\n      - Post-optimal sum adjustment to exactly 180\n      - Bounds [0,1] maintained throughout optimization\n      - C5 computation: Must use full correlation with dx scaling\n   b. Comparison metrics:\n      - Final C5 value to compare against parent's 0.380870\n      - Requires explicit lower bound below 0.3808\n   c. Validation procedures:\n      - Triple-count C5 with different mode implementations\n      - Ensure output samples retain independence of the initial parent\n      - Include consistency checking for all pruning strategies",
